    {
      "duration_ms": 10000,
      "caption": "Besides true dependences, anti, output and memory dependences also constrain the order.",
      "narration": "Other edges matter too. Write-after-read and write-after-write dependences, and possibly aliasing memory operations, also forbid reordering. Registers renamed earlier remove many false edges.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Hazards",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 5,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "l",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "RAW: true data dependence",
            "WAR: anti dependence",
            "WAW: output dependence",
            "Memory: may-alias loads and stores",
            "Side effects and barriers stay ordered"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 18,
          "w": 84,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "color": "orange",
          "at": 1300
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 12500,
      "caption": "List scheduling repeatedly emits the best ready instruction, ranked by critical path.",
      "narration": "List scheduling keeps a ready set. Each cycle it picks the highest-priority instruction, typically the one on the longest latency path, emits it, and releases successors when their latencies elapse.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "List scheduling",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 5,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "ready = nodes with no preds\nwhile ready not empty:\n  n = highest priority(ready)\n  emit(n); cycle++\n  release succs whose\n  latency has elapsed",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 18,
          "w": 84,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "at": 1300
        },
        {
          "id": "hl",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "c",
          "at": 2100
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 11500,
      "caption": "Machine models drive the choices, and llvm-mca shows what the pipeline actually does.",
      "narration": "LLVM describes each CPU's latencies and ports in TableGen scheduling models. Scheduling before register allocation raises pressure, and after it faces false dependences. llvm-mca simulates the result.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "In LLVM",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 5,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "text": "llvm-mca -mcpu=skylake -timeline a.s",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 18,
          "w": 84,
          "enter": "slide-up",
          "at": 1300
        },
        {
          "id": "l",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
